<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Qian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 113 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Qian was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> envoy sent west by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> around 138 BCE to seek an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Held captive by the Xiongnu for years, he reached Dayuan, Kangju and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and returned in 126 BCE; a later mission went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>. His reports gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> its first detailed knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and opened the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-qian.jpg" alt="Zhang Qian" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHG · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhangQianTravels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Dynasty's most consequential contribution to world history was the Silk Road, the network of overland and maritime trade routes connecting China to Central Asia, Persia, Rome, and India, opened by <b>Zhang Qian</b>'s missions and Emperor Wu's western campaigns and sustained by Han military presence across the Tarim Basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Zhang Qian</b>, visiting Bactria about 128 BCE, found bamboo canes from Qiong and cloth from Shu in its markets and was told that merchants bought them in Shendu, northwestern India, a settled farming land like Bactria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its strategic position along the Silk Road facilitated the movement of goods, ideas and technologies between the Roman world and Han China, which first established contact with Parthia through embassies following the mission of <b>Zhang Qian</b> in the second century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han search for allies against the Xiongnu also sent <b>Zhang Qian</b> on his celebrated mission to the Yuezhi in 139 BCE, opening Chinese knowledge of Central Asia and helping to establish the routes later known as the Silk Road.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji of Sima Qian, which draws on the report of the envoy <b>Zhang Qian</b>, they originally lived between Dunhuang and the Qilian Mountains in the Gansu corridor, and in the late third century BCE they were strong enough to look down on the Xiongnu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…passed to the Seleucids and in part to the Greek kings of Bactria, until nomadic peoples from the north and east overran the region in the 2nd century BCE; the Chinese envoy <b>Zhang Qian</b>, who reached Central Asia in the 120s BCE, knew it as part of Kangju.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
